       The ssttrreerrrroorr() function returns a pointer to a  string  that  describes
       the  error  code  passed  in  the  argument  _e_r_r_n_u_m, possibly using the
       LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS part of the current locale to select the  appropriate  lan‐
       guage.   (For  example,  if  _e_r_r_n_u_m is EEIINNVVAALL, the returned description
       will be "Invalid argument".)  This string must not be modified  by  the
       application,  and  the returned pointer will be invalidated on a subse‐
       quent call to ssttrreerrrroorr() or ssttrreerrrroorr__ll(), or if  the  thread  that  ob‐
       tained  the  string  exits.   No other library function, including ppeerr‐‐
       rroorr(3), will modify this string.

       Like ssttrreerrrroorr(), the ssttrreerrrroorrddeesscc__nnpp() function returns a pointer to  a
       string  that  describes  the  error code passed in the argument _e_r_r_n_u_m,
       with the difference that the returned string is not translated  accord‐
       ing to the current locale.

       The ssttrreerrrroorrnnaammee__nnpp() function returns a pointer to a string containing
       the name of the error code passed in the argument _e_r_r_n_u_m.  For example,
       given  EEPPEERRMM  as  an  argument,  this function returns a pointer to the
       string "EPERM".  Given 00  as  an  argument,  this  function  returns  a
       pointer to the string "0".

